INT. FAMILY SEWING ROOM - ATTIC - LATE AFTERNOON

Dust turns slowly in a shaft of low gold light. A dress form wears half a wedding gown, pins bristling at the hem like a porcupine. Bolts of fabric lean in the corners, patient as old dogs.

AGATHA FENWICK (60s, wire-rimmed glasses on a beaded chain, thimble on her middle finger) kneels at the hem, working a needle through satin. Her daughter ROSAMUND (30s, stiff-backed, coat still on, car keys unreleased in her fist) stands in the doorway as though the floor might be hot.

                    AGATHA
          You're early.

                    ROSAMUND
          Traffic was light.

                    AGATHA
          (to the hem)
          Hold still, you.

She isn't speaking to the dress form. Rosamund does not know whether to answer anyway.

                    ROSAMUND
          Is that Lena's?

                    AGATHA
          It was going to be. I'm altering it.

                    ROSAMUND
          For who?

Agatha bites the thread. Does not reply. She stands with a small grunt, takes a step back, and squints at the gown with the eye of a general.

                    AGATHA
          Everyone, take a seat. Please. The fitting's about to start.

The room has no one else in it. Rosamund looks over her shoulder anyway, at the empty hall.

                    ROSAMUND
          Mum.

                    AGATHA
          (muttering)
          Too long. Always too long. I told her.

                    ROSAMUND
          It's mine, isn't it?

The needle stops mid-air.

                    AGATHA
          You said you'd never wear it.

                    ROSAMUND
          I said I'd never get married.

                    AGATHA
          Same thing, the way you said it.

                    ROSAMUND
          You altered a dress in secret for eleven weeks and you want to talk about how I said it?

                    AGATHA
          I wanted it to fit.

Rosamund drops her keys. They hit the floorboards with a crack that makes the dress form seem to flinch.

                    ROSAMUND
          You never once asked me what I wanted.

                    AGATHA
          You never once told me!

The shout hangs. Dust spins. Rosamund steps forward, picks up the hem, and holds the satin in her fist.

                    ROSAMUND
          (quietly)
          Pin it shorter.
